+ * guest that spin-reads a report without ever flipping, where neither
+ * valve would trip because one outstanding query grows neither the queue
+ * nor the pool. Worst case there is 100 ms of added latency on that read,
+ * not a hang, so this is a latency choice and not a correctness one.
+ */
+/*
+ * Corrected 2026-11-05, and this one was a plain logic error. There used
+ * to be a second valve here: drain early if the report queue is deeper
+ * than 256, on the reasoning that a deep queue means a lot of outstanding
+ * work. Timing the drains showed it firing 100 to 127 times per 5 s in
+ * heavy combat and costing 1656 to 2408 ms of that 5 s, between a third
+ * and a half of the entire GPU stall budget, while the pool valve never
+ * fired once and queries in flight never passed 128 of 1024.
+ *
+ * The reasoning was backwards. A guest spinning on one result has a queue
+ * one deep and must be served; a guest with 300 queued is firing and
+ * forgetting and is not waiting for any of them. So a deep queue is a
+ * reason to defer, not a reason to drain. It was arguing for urgency from
+ * the evidence of no urgency.
+ *
+ * What remains is the query pool, which is a fixed 1024 and asserts on
+ * overflow, so that valve is a real limit rather than a guess; and the
+ * breaker below, for a guest that spin reads without ever flipping. The
+ * floor is unchanged: every flip finishes and every finish empties the
+ * whole queue.
